Description:
  The tab strip shared by every screen in the Courses group: the course list, the two halves of the
  taxonomy, and the two enrolment queues.

  It exists because these five belong together and the main navigation cannot say so on its own. A
  theme renders one menu item and a flat list of children, so a group heading in the menu is a change
  every theme has to implement, including ones this project does not maintain. A tab strip inside the
  page body is core-owned, needs no theme cooperation, and puts the whole group one click apart.

  Every screen in the group renders this in the same place: immediately above its own heading, before
  anything else in the section body. That is what makes it read as one interface rather than as five
  pages that happen to link to each other, and it is why the strip does not move or change contents
  depending on which of the five the reader is on.

  These are links, not buttons, and each has its own route. They used to be in-page tab buttons for
  requests and enrolments, which meant neither could be linked to, bookmarked or reloaded on its own,
  and the browser's back button could not return to the one the reader had been looking at.

  A tab the reader cannot use is not shown at all rather than shown disabled: the strip is a map of
  where this operator can go, and a dead entry on it is a map of somebody else's platform.

  WARNING: a template comment is not a safe place to document syntax. A comment delimiter
  written inside a comment body ends the comment early and leaks the remainder onto the page.
  Keep every comment plain prose: no comment delimiters and no code, not even to document one.
Changelog:
